Presidential elections were held in [[Croatia]] on 29 December 2024, with a second round held on 12 January 2025 after no candidate received a majority of the vote in the first round. Incumbent president [[Zoran Milanović]] was eligible for a second term,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Croatia |url=https://www.fao.org/faolex/results/details/es/c/LEX-FAOC129771/ |access-date=2023-10-07 |website=FAO}}</ref> and was re-elected with a record 75% of the vote.

== Background ==
Voters in Croatia had also voted for the [[Croatian Parliament]] in [[2024 Croatian parliamentary election|April]] and [[European Parliament]] in [[2024 European Parliament election in Croatia|June]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 January 2024 |title=Superizborna godina za Hrvate: Birače očekuju europski, parlamentarni i predsjednički izbori |trans-title=Super election year for Croats: European, parliamentary and presidential elections await voters |url=https://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/superizborna-godina-za-hrvate-birace-ocekuju-europski-parlamentarni-i-predsjednicki-izbori-1735584 |access-date=16 March 2024 |website=Večernji list |language=hr}}</ref>

The first round was planned for December 2024 but on 15 March 2024 incumbent president [[Zoran Milanović]] announced he would run as a candidate of the [[Social Democratic Party of Croatia]] (SDP) for the position of [[Prime Minister of Croatia|prime minister]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 March 2024 |title=Milanović ide na izbore sa SDP-om: Bit će kandidat za premijera |trans-title=Milanović is going to the elections with SDP: He will be a candidate for prime minister |url=https://dnevnik.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/zoran-milanovic-otkrio-da-ide-na-izbore-i-s-kim---838148.html |access-date=16 March 2024 |website=Dnevnik.hr |language=hr}}</ref> During this press conference, he also revealed he would tend his resignation as the [[President of Croatia|president]] only in case of a [[2024 Croatian parliamentary election|parliamentary election]] victory on 17 April 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 March 2024 |title=Oglasio se Milanović nakon šokantne objave: Otkrio kada će odstupiti s mjesta predsjednika |trans-title=Milanović spoke after the shocking announcement: Revealed when he will step down as president |url=https://dnevnik.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/zoran-milanovic-otkrio-kada-ce-odstupiti-s-mjesta-predsjednika---838159.html |access-date=15 March 2024 |website=Dnevnik.hr |language=hr}}</ref> Had Milanović become prime minister, the constitution required that a preliminary presidential election taking place no later than 16 June 2024. However, the SDP-led [[Rivers of Justice]] coalition won only 42 seats in the election and the HDZ won a vote of confidence after securing the backing of minority representatives and several MPs from the [[Homeland Movement (Croatia)|Homeland Movement]], with Milanović continuing as president.

On 20 November 2024, Prime Minister [[Andrej Plenković]] announced that the presidential election would be held on 29 December, which was approved by the [[Croatian Parliament]] on 21 November.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 November 2024 |title=Croatia to hold a presidential election on Dec. 29 |url=https://apnews.com/article/croatia-presidential-election-december-fd7e767130deff4b9e4f8a379f0db2ea |access-date=20 November 2024 |website=Associated Press |language=en}}</ref>

The leadup to the election was marred by several controversies, including the November arrest and subsequent dismissal of Minister of Health [[Vili Beroš]] on various corruption-related charges.<ref name="beros">{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=15 November 2024 |title=The investigation against Beroš and seven others was initiated by the EPPO |url=https://n1info.hr/english/news/the-investigation-against-beros-and-seven-others-was-initiated-by-the-eppo/ |access-date=15 November 2024 |website=[[N1 (Croatia)|N1]] |language=hr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=15 November 2024 |title=A Croatian corruption investigation snares the health minister and several others |url=https://apnews.com/article/croatia-arrest-corruption-minister-health-procurement-hospitals-d9f35a6aebc9a2462bf8e102f51aa4f7 |access-date=15 November 2024 |website=Associated Press |language=en}}</ref> This caused a standoff between Croatian law enforcement agency [[USKOK]] and the [[European Public Prosecutor's Office]], one of whose investigations was blown by the arrest. [[DORH|Attorney General of Croatia]] [[Ivan Turudić]] ruled that USKOK would continue the investigation using the evidence collected by EPPO. Milanović criticised this decision.<ref name="beros" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=HDZ-ov dečko Turudić: Istrage EPPO-a o Berošu više nema. Sve moraju prijaviti USKOK-u |url=https://www.index.hr/vijesti/clanak/hdzov-decko-turudic-istrage-eppoa-o-berosu-vise-nema-sve-moraju-prijaviti-uskoku/2616902.aspx |access-date=2024-11-20 |website=Index.hr |language=hr}}</ref> Another controversy happened around the same time when Milanović vetoed the government's proposal to involve Croatia in a NATO operation training Ukrainian soldiers. The parliament did not attempt to overrule his veto.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Devčić |first1=Matej |title=Hoće li Sabor ikada glasati o famoznoj misiji NSATU? Sve je više indicija da HDZ tu temu želi zaboraviti |url=https://www.telegram.hr/politika-kriminal/hoce-li-sabor-ikada-glasati-o-famoznoj-misiji-nsatu-sve-je-vise-indicija-da-hdz-tu-temu-zeli-zaboraviti/ |website=[[Telegram.hr]] |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=12 December 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Devčić |first1=Matej |title=Milanović: 'Znamo tko su nam saveznici, to je zapadni svijet. Ali nisu nam gospodari' |url=https://www.telegram.hr/politika-kriminal/milanovic-znamo-tko-su-nam-saveznici-to-je-zapadni-svijet-ali-nisu-nam-gospodari/ |website=Telegram.hr |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=22 November 2024}}</ref>

== Electoral system ==
The [[president of Croatia]] is directly elected by secret ballot to a term of five years using the [[two-round system]], with presidents limited to two full terms in office. The [[Constitution of Croatia|constitution]] requires that a presidential election be held no sooner than 60 days and no later than 30 days before the expiration of the incumbent president's term. An absolute majority (50% + 1 vote) of all votes cast (including invalid, blank and uncast ballots) is required to win in the first round. If no candidate receives a majority of the vote, a second-round is to be held fourteen days later, with the two candidates with the highest number of votes in the first round taking part. The candidate who receives the highest number of votes in the second round (a majority of valid votes cast) is declared the winner. If one of the candidates who has qualified for the second round were to withdraw their candidacy or die, the candidate with the next highest number of votes in the first round takes their place in the second round.<ref name="Propisi">[http://www.sabor.hr/ustav-republike-hrvatske Važniji propisi] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161213070728/http://www.sabor.hr/ustav-republike-hrvatske|date=2016-12-13}} Croatian Parliament {{in lang|hr}}</ref>

In order for a potential candidate to be allowed to contest the elections and have their name placed on the ballot, they must gather at least 10,000 signatures from eligible voters, with every signatory being permitted to give their signature to only one potential candidate. The timeframe for collecting the said number of signatures is set at twelve days, and after the expiry of this period potential candidates must submit them to the State Electoral Commission for verification.<ref>{{cite web |date=20 November 2014 |title=Bliže se predsjednički izbori! Ovo su datumi koje morate znati. |trans-title=The presidential election is getting near! These are the dates you have to know. |url=https://dnevnik.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/ovo-su-svi-datumi-koje-morate-znati-za-nadolazece-predsjednicke-izbore---361563.html |access-date=22 December 2018 |publisher=[[Dnevnik.hr]] |language=hr}}</ref>

