==Campaign==
[[File:Milanovićev predizborni plakat (cropped).jpg|thumb|Milanović's campaign poster with a message: "Normally – the Croatian soldier will not wage wars of others'."]]
The incumbent, Zoran Milanović, ran on an anti-corruption campaign, opposing social inequality and growing inflation, which he attributed to policies of the HDZ government. He said that his key values are "the rule of law, right to equality, personal freedoms and rights of minorities". On foreign policy, Milanović said he would "keep Croatia outside global conflicts," and that the country must "retain the right to independent decisions about its national interests". Milanović said he would not slow former Yugoslav countries' accession to the EU.<ref name="milanović net.hr">{{cite web |last1=Rebac |first1=Iva |title=Istodobno mu se dive i kritiziraju ga: Zoran Milanović, političar koji nikoga ne ostavlja ravnodušnim |url=https://www.net.hr/danas/vijesti/tko-je-zoran-milanovic-i-sto-nudi-u-svom-izbornom-programu-fcd85d56-b0a7-11ef-9db7-829ff0affdfc |website=Net.hr |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr-HR |date=9 December 2024}}</ref>

Dragan Primorac ran on a campaign of co-operation with the HDZ government.<ref name="hdz">{{cite web |title=Pismo naciji Dragana Primorca |url=https://hdz.hr/article/4842/pismo-naciji-dragana-primorca |publisher=Croatian Democratic Union (HDZ) |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=10 January 2025 |quote=... poštovati Ustav i predano raditi na vraćanju nacionalnog jedinstva, umjesto podjela koje razaraju naše društvo}}</ref> He said he would "return civility ... particularly to political and public spheres of life" and that "the military is always above politics", and he offered "unconditional adherence" to "Western values" and the NATO alliance. Primorac's campaign was conservative on regional issues. He promised to "offer special attention to the rights" of [[Croats in Bosnia and Herzegovina]],<ref name="primorac hrt">{{cite web |title=Primorac objavio kandidaturu za predsjednika RH, HDZ mu dao potporu |url=https://vijesti.hrt.hr/hrvatska/dragan-primorac-objavio-kandidaturu-za-predsjednicke-izbore-11686143 |publisher=[[Croatian Radiotelevision]] |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=31 July 2024}}</ref> and prevent [[Serbia]] from [[accession of Serbia to the European Union|joining the EU]] unless it provided Croatia with access to archives containing information about mass graves from the [[Croatian War of Independence]] (1991&ndash;1995).<ref name="primorac 24sata">{{cite web |agency=[[HINA]] |title=Primorac: 'Zakočit ću ulazak Srbije u EU ako ne otvori arhive' |url=https://www.24sata.hr/news/primorac-zakocit-cu-ulazak-srbije-u-eu-ako-ne-otvori-arhive-1021697 |website=[[24 sata (Croatia)|24 sata]] |access-date=16 January 2025 |location=Zagreb |language=hr |date=14 December 2024}}</ref>

Neither Primorac nor Milanović led an active campaign.<ref name="macan net.hr">{{cite web |title=Krešimir Macan komentirao strategiju za predsjedničke izbore Zorana Milanovića |url=https://www.net.hr/danas/rtl-direkt/kresimir-macan-komentirao-strategiju-za-predsjednicke-izbore-zorana-milanovica-795b3110-adc8-11ef-8019-b282ec61614a |website=Net.hr |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=28 November 2024}}</ref> Milanović attended only a single debate, on 23 December, during the first round of the elections,<ref name="debate 1">{{cite web |last1=Matijević |first1=Božena |title=Debata godine: Milanović dolazi samo na sučeljavanje na HTV-u |url=https://vecernji.hr/vijesti/debata-godine-milanovic-dolazi-samo-na-suceljavanje-na-htv-u-1824271 |website=[[Večernji list]] |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=18 December 2024}}</ref> but his ratings remained high due to the popularity of his presidency. According to political analysts, both Milanović and Primorac expected to enter the second round regardless of their campaigns.<ref name="macan net.hr"/>

Marija Selak Raspudić campaigned on a platform that included economic problems, population decline and corruption. Ivana Kekin campaigned against Dragan Primorac and the HDZ, accusing them of corruption in the healthcare sector.<ref>{{cite web |title=Croatia's outspoken president is seeking reelection. He faces several contenders in Sunday's vote |url=https://apnews.com/article/croatia-president-election-milanovic-73147e652adbbfb3a9bdb3de89316122 |website=Associated Press |date=27 December 2024 |access-date=27 December 2024}}</ref>{{Better source needed|date=January 2025}} On 7 December a group of Croatian veterans from [[Imotski]] held a peaceful protest at the [[Vinjani Donji]]–[[Gorica, Grude|Gorica]] border crossing against what they claimed was a "media blockade of candidate Tomislav Jonjić".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dalmacijadanas.hr/blokadom-na-blokadu-imotskim-braniteljima-prekipjelo-izisli-na-granicu-ljuti-ih-tretman-prema-jednom-predsjednickom-kandidatu-spremni-smo-na-totalnu-blokadu|title= BLOKADOM NA BLOKADU Imotskim braniteljima prekipjelo, izišli na granicu, ljuti ih tretman prema jednom predsjedničkom kandidatu: "Spremni smo na totalnu blokadu"|website=dalmacijadanas.hr|publisher=Dalmacija Danas|language=hr|date=7 December 2024}}</ref>

In the first round Primorac trailed Milanović by 30 percentage points. In the only debate of the second round debate, on 7 January, both candidates turned to insults.<ref name="debate 2">{{cite web |title=Evo kada i gdje će biti jedina predsjednička debata drugog kruga, odazvali se i Primorac i Milanović |url=https://n1info.hr/vijesti/odazvali-se-milanovic-i-primorac-poznato-kada-i-gdje-ce-biti-jedina-predsjednicka-debata-drugog-kruga/ |website=N1 |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=2 January 2025}}</ref><ref name="urdu point">{{cite web |title=Croatia To Vote After Insult-filled Presidential Race |url=https://www.urdupoint.com/en/world/croatia-to-vote-after-insult-filled-president-1900045.html |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]] |work=[[UrduPoint]] |access-date=16 January 2025 |location=Pakistan |language=en |date=27 December 2024}}</ref> Milanović accused Primorac of being "totally illiterate", while Primorac called Milanović a "pro-Russian puppet" who "divided" Croatia.<ref>{{cite web |title=Croatia's populist president tipped for re-election |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20250110-croatia-s-populist-president-tipped-for-re-election |website=France 24 |access-date=10 January 2025}}</ref> On the same day, the ''[[Jutarnji list]]'' newspaper prematurely ended an online election poll after receiving 25,000 votes tipping the scale to Primorac in a short timespan from US-based internet addresses.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Raknić |first1=Damjan |title=Objavili smo anketu u kojoj smo vas pitali za koga ćete glasati. Onda nam je stiglo više od 25.000 glasova iz – SAD-a! |url=https://www.jutarnji.hr/vijesti/hrvatska/objavili-smo-anketu-u-kojoj-smo-vas-pitali-za-koga-cete-glasati-onda-nam-je-stiglo-vise-od-25-000-glasova-iz-sad-a-15540432 |website=[[Jutarnji list]] |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=7 January 2025}}</ref> In the wake of the debate, the Centre for Information Resilience, a UK-based organisation, published a report alleging that "pro-Russian, anti-EU, and anti-NATO bot networks"<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.info-res.org/cir/articles/disinformation-campaign-uncovered-by-researchers-ahead-of-croatian-presidential-run-off/ | title=Disinformation campaign uncovered by researchers ahead of Croatian presidential run-off }}</ref> promoting Milanović on several social media networks were attempting to influence elections. However, the ''[[Večernji list]]'' national newspaper managed to contact the owner of one of the [[Facebook]] accounts singled out in the report. The account turned out to belong to a real person, a Facebook activist from [[Dugo Selo]] near [[Zagreb]].<ref name="vecernji uk">{{cite web |last1=Prerad |first1=Danijel |title=Javila nam se Zagrepčanka koju su Britanci optužili da je ruski bot: U šoku je |url=https://www.vecernji.hr/vijesti/javila-nam-se-zagrepbcanka-koju-su-britanci-optuzili-da-je-ruski-bot-u-soku-je-1829237 |website=[[Večernji list]] |access-date=16 January 2025 |location=Zagreb |language=hr |date=9 January 2025}}</ref> Social media networks [[Twitter|X]] and [[TikTok]] both denied the allegations; TikTok reported removing a different bot network promoting Primorac.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Menđušić |first1=Silvana |title=Najniža točka kampanje za 2. krug na lokaciji masovne grobnice |url=https://www.telegram.hr/politika-kriminal/milanovic-kampanju-jedva-da-je-i-vodio-a-hdz-za-primorca-upregnuo-bas-sve-najniza-tocka-zabiljezena-je-kod-vukovara-na-lokaciji-moguce-masovne-grobnice/ |website=Telegram.hr |access-date=16 January 2025 |language=hr |date=10 January 2025}}</ref>

